A||||6||-=shame=-||AC belt||||5||
Z||000000||-=shame=-||06-29-2000||06:56 AM||||Hey, can the A/C belt be changed from underneath on a '86 v6. From what I saw, there is a peice of metal bolted to the frame which it's only purpose must be to deflect road debri. Was hoping I could losen the alternator, remove the S-belt and put the A/C belt on from below. Just wanna know if anyone has tried this? Don't know if I'll have enough room to tighten the belt once on.||||reg||5
Z||000001||ThatRickGuy||06-29-2000||07:09 AM||||I was just thinking of how much easier it would be if the fire wall was hinged so that it could be opened from inside the passanger compartment. If i weren't warried about the structural strength I go ahead and do it. but anyways, if you're talking about that little piece of sheet metal on the engine cradle, I &gt;think&lt; it's tack welded in place. I remeber dealing with it when I was pulling the egnine from my car and I don't remember any bolts on it.||||reg||1
Z||000002||TOM||06-29-2000||10:57 AM||||I just removed and replaced my ac compressor on a V6. Yes you need to do it from underneath. I didnt run into any sheet metal that was welded. All the shileds I removed were bolted.||||reg||1
Z||000003||DJRice||06-29-2000||12:14 PM||||If I recall correctly, there are only 2 10mm bolts holding it on, but it does catch all the road debris so they might be hard to see.||||reg||1
Z||000004||StuGood||06-29-2000||12:33 PM||||1986 V6?  I have two of those.  You can do this job any way you want to, of course.  But my recommendation is this:<P>Loosen lug nuts on RR wheel.  Jack up / support RHS of car (unless you're extremely skinny, you'll have to do this to get underneath any way).  Remove RR wheel.<BR>  <BR>Remove plastic push-in fasteners and bolts from forward half of plastic inner splash shield (inner fender), and "fold" it out of your way.  It really ISN'T hard to do this!  Use a miniature pry bar or nail puller to release the push-in fasteners (pry out the inner "button," then pry fastener out of the hole in the substructure).  Store all fasteners in an old vitamin container, so they don't get lost. <P>Now you can see the belts/pulleys on the "front" (actually RHS) side of engine.  Remove alternator belt.  Remove A/C belt. Clean up/ resolve any oil leakage problems.<P>Clean and paint timing mark on harmonic balancer while you're in there.  I use a swatch of screaming orange enamel on either side of the timing slot.  You'll be glad you did this, next time (pun intended)   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>(Beware - there is more than 1 slot on the damper!  The one you want to mark is the widest one). <BR> <BR>Install A/C belt; tighten using giant screwdriver thru slots in bracket from underneath.  Install and tighten alternator belt using neat-o belt tightener gizmo from JCW (or Harbor Freight Store - faster and cheaper), as mentioned in previous threads.<P>Reinstall inner fender, tire, etc., lower car to ground, and torque lug nuts.<P>That's what Stu would do. Sounds more involved than it really is, 'cause Stu is a windy cuss, once he gets started   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">.<P>Now go do whatever you want   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">.  Hope this helps   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">.<P>BTW, there are indeed (2) 10mm nuts holding  that sheet metal "corner" piece to the cradle, as the previous post states.  I remember that much.                <p>[This message has been edited by StuGood (edited 06-29-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||-=shame=-||06-29-2000||08:04 PM||||So Stu I gather I don't need to free that rusted hunk of tin from my frame. But, the markings for timing is that to return the belt to a specific location or is it just that I may need it later on? Or, you're just pullin my leg.||||reg||6
Z||000006||StuGood||06-29-2000||11:06 PM||||Sorry about the confusion  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"> !<P>If that "rusty piece of tin" is in your way, remove it.  It's just two bolts.<P>As far as cleaning/marking the timing mark on the damper, it has nothing to do with installing the belts - it's just that it makes setting your ignition timing a breeze -whether you plan on doing it yourself, or have someone else do it.  Your mechanic will appreciate people like you  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">.<P>Trying to clean and mark the damper is so easy while you have the inner fender & belts off, this is the time to do it.<P>Reminds me -! Another thing to do while the pulley is so accessible is to check the slack in your timing chain (a commonly known wear item in these engines).<P>Turn the crank with a socket (19mm?)and breaker bar CCW to take up the slack on one side; continue to turn until timing mark shows 0 degree TDC. Then turn it CW until you feel/hear the chain tighten up inside ("clank!").  However far the crank turned (from TDC to "clank") tells you how much slack is in the chain.        <P>MUCH easier than trying to observe the distributor turning.<P>No kidding.   ||||reg||9
